                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "It's interesting to see where it all began for Frictional Games. You can feel the beginnings of what would eventually become Amnesia throughout. The mournful, oppressive sound design, the physics puzzles, the note-based storytelling, the frantic chase sequences, it's all hear in more or less rough form. Even more than The Dark Descent, you can see the connection back to classic 90s puzzle/adventure games, with a lot of the exploration and puzzles feeling almost like a haunted house version of something like Myst.\r\n\r\nWeird that this game has combat in it, and it immediately becomes apparent why they discontinued that element for their future games. What should feel tense and visceral quickly turns into a \"stand on a box and hit dogs that can't reach you\" simulator. Trying to actually engage with the combat as intended is miserably clunky, it practically begs you to cheese the encounters so you can just move on.\r\n\r\nAs with all Frictional games, the music is a huge highlight. The main menu theme with the rainfall ambience is haunting, and the more intense version of the same theme that plays during the end credits is also amazing.",
